macOS: do not let the java_home probe depend on reaping its own child

_java_home_macos() forks /usr/libexec/java_home, reads the path off a pipe,
then waitpid()s for it and rejects the answer unless the exit status is zero:

    int fd[2], exitcode;
    ...
    waitpid(java_home_pid, &exitcode, 0);

    if (result[0] == '\0' || exitcode) {

exitcode is never initialised, and waitpid() only writes it when it succeeds.
An application that reaps its own children -- anything with a SIGCHLD handler
or a signal thread doing waitpid(-1, ...) -- collects this child first, so
libbluray's waitpid() returns -1/ECHILD and the test reads uninitialised stack.
Whatever that garbage happens to be decides whether BD-J works.

VLC is exactly such an application: on macOS it blocks SIGCHLD in every thread
and dequeues it on a dedicated thread that loops on waitpid(-1, &status,
WNOHANG) (bin/darwinvlc.m). Measured against a BD-J disc: the player reported

    Unable to read path from /usr/libexec/java_home
    BD-J check: Failed to load JVM library
    BD-J menus not supported. Playing without menus. BD-J support: 1, JVM found: 0

while libbluray's own bd_info, run on the same disc with the same library and
the same JDK a second earlier, printed "Java VM found: yes". The only
difference is the reaper thread. It is a race, so it is intermittent -- which
is worse than a clean failure, because it also occasionally works.

The exit status is not needed to tell success from failure: java_home prints a
path on stdout and nothing at all when it has no JDK to offer, and the empty
check above already covers that. So default exitcode to 0 and treat a failed
waitpid() as "someone else already collected it", keeping the status check for
the case where the child really was ours and really did fail.

Also terminate the execl() argument list. execl() takes a NULL-terminated
varargs list and this call passes none, so the callee reads past the end of the
arguments -- undefined behaviour that happens to work only as long as the stack
slot after argv[0] is zero.

--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
@@ -249,7 +249,7 @@
         return result;

     pid_t java_home_pid;
-    int fd[2], exitcode;
+    int fd[2], exitcode = 0;

     if (pipe(fd)) {
         BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "unable to set up pipes\n");
@@ -270,7 +270,7 @@
             close(fd[1]);
             close(fd[0]);

-            execl(MACOS_JAVA_HOME, MACOS_JAVA_HOME);
+            execl(MACOS_JAVA_HOME, MACOS_JAVA_HOME, (char *)NULL);

             _exit(-1);

@@ -285,7 +285,13 @@
                 result[len-1] = '\0';
             }

-            waitpid(java_home_pid, &exitcode, 0);
+            if (waitpid(java_home_pid, &exitcode, 0) < 0) {
+                /* The host application reaps its own children (a SIGCHLD
+                 * handler, or a signal thread calling waitpid(-1, ...)) and
+                 * got there first. The status is gone; the path we just read
+                 * off the pipe is what matters, and it is checked below. */
+                exitcode = 0;
+            }
     }

     if (result[0] == '\0' || exitcode) {
